    "collected_at": "2026-11-09T20:22:22.924239+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.biospace.com/drug-development/details-from-novartis-failed-cardiovascular-trial-offer-little-silver-lining-for-rivals/#article",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.biospace.com/drug-development/details-from-novartis-failed-cardiovascular-trial-offer-little-silver-lining-for-rivals",
    "origin_publisher": "BioSpace",
    "published_at": "2026-11-09T15:34:40.807000+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-09T15:34:40.807Z",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-09T20:36:12.897270+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-09T20:36:12.897270+00:00",
    "body": "Full data from the Phase 3 HORIZON trial of Novartis’ pelacarsen failed to show any apparent benefit, leading analysts to further reduce the odds of success for Lp(a)-lowering competitors such as Amgen’s olpasiran.\nAfter Novartis and Ionis Pharmaceuticals reported that pelacarsen failed to improve cardiovascular outcomes in a late-stage test, analysts had held out hope that the details would still leave some room for other therapies that target Lp(a). But now, with the fine print revealed, industry watchers see a tough path ahead for the space.\n“HORIZON’s full data offer little silver lining,” Truist Securities analysts said in a Sunday evening note.\nWhile the antisense oligonucleotide greatly lowered levels of lipoprotein(a)—aka, the bad cholesterol—HORIZON found no benefit in terms of major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE), according to the company’s presentation at the 2026 American Heart Association Scientific Sessions on Saturday and a simultaneous publication in The New England Journal of Medicine ( NEJM ). The trial featured 8,300 adults with cardiovascular disease who were followed for seven years.\nInvestors had been focused on the full HORIZON dataset for possible trends pointing toward a benefit in cardiovascular outcomes “that could have read-through to ongoing trials with siRNA and oral molecules that have greater Lp(a) lowering capacity,” William Blair analysts wrote in a Monday morning note, but “HORIZON cut many ways remains negative.”\nAmong patients with high Lp(a), a MACE event—which includes nonfatal heart attack, stroke, an urgent revascularization procedure to open clogged heart arteries or death from any cardiovascular cause—occurred in 380 participants (9.1%) treated with pelacarsen compared to 434 participants (10.4%) on placebo, according to a subgroup analysis published in NEJM Evidence .\nWilliam Blair pointed to the high rate of discontinuations among participants taking pelacarsen as “one major caveat.” In HORIZON, 1,462 patients (35.1%) given 80-mg pelacarsen every four weeks prematurely discontinued treatment, the analyst said, vs. 761 (18.3%) given placebo on this timeframe.\nBut even with the discontinuations out of the equation, the drug still failed. For patients treated with uninterrupted pelacarsen, 167 or 11% had a MACE event vs. 204 or 10.1% in the placebo group, according to the subgroup analysis.\nCardiovascular disease\nNovartis’ cholesterol-lowering medicine fails Phase 3, clouding Lp(a) horizons\nThe failure of Novartis’ pelacarsen to translate its Lp(a)-lowering effects to clinical benefits “marks a meaningful setback” for other companies taking a similar approach to cardiovascular risk, analysts say—including Eli Lilly and Amgen.\nSeptember 8, 2026\n·\n2 min read\n·\nTristan Manalac\nRead more\n“This discontinuation rate is higher than we expected heading into the Lp(a)HORIZON readout and may have censored data impacting the primary endpoint analysis,” William Blair said.\nNovartis and Ionis revealed in September that the trial failed to meet the primary endpoint of lowering the risk of MACE vs. placebo but did not provide details at the time.\nThe revelation shook Novartis’ stock amid several other clinical stumbles, with the market damage spreading to Lp(a)-targeting peers Amgen and Eli Lilly. It also raised questions about the value of targeting Lp(a) in cardiovascular disease (CVD). Amgen’s olpasiran and Lilly’s lepodisiran are both siRNA therapies that lower Lp(a) expression.\n“We do see meaningful risk to a potential future in Lp(a)-driven CVD trials in light of results from Lp(a) HORIZON,” William Blair said at the time.\nUpon seeing the detailed results, Leerink Partners lowered the probability of success for olpasiran from 50% to 20%. “[W]e are not lowering odds to 0% because it has a different structure and mechanism,” they wrote in a note to investors on Saturday, adding that Amgen’s drug “drives deeper Lp(a) lowering.”\nWilliam Blair had previously modeled sales of $710 million by 2030 for olpasiran but has now removed these estimates from its updated model. “[W]e still struggle to find confidence in olpasiran based on the totality of data presented,” the firm wrote on Monday.\nEven when looking at the secondary prevention setting—in patients with established CVD—“there was no apparent difference in total events between participants randomly assigned to pelacarsen or placebo,” the NEJM Evidence authors wrote.\nDrug Development\nUpdate: FDA investigates Novartis’ Zolgensma following 9 cases of tumors in treated patients\nAfter Novartis confirmed the reports of tumors in patients who have been treated with the spinal muscular atrophy gene therapy, the FDA will determine if any regulatory action is needed for Zolgensma or for the pharma’s similar product Itvisma.\nNovember 9, 2026\n·\n4 min read\n·\nHeather McKenzie\nRead more\nWilliam Blair spoke specifically to this point, writing, “With no demonstration of efficacy in these high Lp(a) reduction subgroups . . . we believe it will be challenging for future studies to demonstrate benefit in secondary prevention in an otherwise well-controlled population.”\nFor now, the Lp(a) field will have to wait to learn more about whether this approach is viable. The Phase 3 OCEAN(a) study of olpasiran is set to read out topline data in early 2028, while Lilly is anticipating initial data from lepodisiran’s Phase 3 ACCLAIM-Lp(a) trial in March 2029.\nFor Novartis, the public reminder of pelacarsen’s failure couldn’t come at a worse time. Last week, the company confirmed nine reported cases of tumors in patients treated with its spinal muscular atrophy treatment Zolgensma, including two cases where that have been linked by evidence to the gene therapy. The FDA on Friday announced an investigation into the risk of tumor formation associated with the therapy.\nAnd these headlines follow a summer of cascading challenges for Novartis. In September, the antibody-oligonucleotide conjugate del-desiran failed in a Phase 3 trial to significantly improve hand function in patients with myotonic dystrophy type 1, calling into question the company’s $12 billion buyout of Avidity Biosciences last year.\nThen in late August, news broke that Novartis had paused a clutch of Phase 2 trials for the CAR T cell therapy rapcabtagene autoleucel after three patient deaths.\nEditorial\nAfter Novartis’ devastating M&A losses, long live the executive willing to try\nBig Pharma needs more—not fewer—bold bets on rare and difficult diseases after Novartis’ efforts to invest in muscular dystrophy and ALS hit major roadblocks this month.\nSeptember 17, 2026\n·\n2 min read\n·\nAnnalee Armstrong\nRead more",
